<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Grand Principality of Tver</h1><p class="st-meta">1247 CE – 1485 CE · capital Tver</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Moscow's great rival for leadership of north-eastern Russia, whose princes held the throne of Vladimir before Moscow annexed Tver in 1485.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tver, on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, received its first prince around 1247, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-of-tver">Yaroslav Yaroslavich</a>, a younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, made it his seat. Yaroslav became grand prince of Vladimir in 1264, and Tver quickly grew into one of the strongest towns of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal</a> lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tver controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> trade and built early stone churches and fortifications. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-of-tver">Mikhail Yaroslavich</a> became grand prince of Vladimir in 1305 but was executed at the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> in 1318 after a struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. In 1327 a revolt in Tver killed the Mongol envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chol-khan">Cholkhan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan Kalita of Moscow</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> in crushing it. The merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afanasy-nikitin">Afanasy Nikitin</a> set out from Tver for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 1460s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-of-tver">Boris Alexandrovich</a> Tver revived in the 15th century, but it was surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> territory. When its last prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-borisovich-of-tver">Mikhail Borisovich</a>, sought Lithuanian help, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> besieged the city and annexed it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> in 1485.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/grand-principality-of-tver.jpg" alt="Grand Principality of Tver" width="320" height="263"><figcaption>Image: Hellerick · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Principality_of_Tver_14th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1247 CE – 1485 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tver</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mikhail Yaroslavich, Alexander Mikhailovich, Boris Alexandrovich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Tver, Tver</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1366&amp;focus=grand-principality-of-tver" aria-label="Grand Principality of Tver on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5455 248 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Grand Principality of Tver lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5775 504L5878 523M5775 504L6352 637M5775 504L5820 533M5775 504L5807 490"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5878 523h0M6352 637h0M5820 533h0M5807 490h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5775 504h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5775 504h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tver. <a href="/atlas?year=1366&amp;focus=grand-principality-of-tver">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
